@prefix lr:   <https://loopingrules.org/ns#> .
@prefix tut:  <https://loopingrules.org/tutorial#> .
@prefix ex:   <https://example.org/> .

# The tutorial's own state: where you are, and the lessons. It's data like any other.
# tut: is the tutorial's machinery; ex: is your world.

tut:progress tut:current tut:L0 .

# The first message is part of the seed, so it shows up before anything runs.
tut:welcome lr:channel lr:out ; a lr:Say ;
  lr:text """Welcome to LoopingRules!

This tutorial runs itself, as rules. To start it, tick "listen" in the top bar, then press ▶ Autopilot. From then on, the tutorial reacts to every edit you make and every message you send.""" .

# --- lessons -------------------------------------------------------------------

tut:L0 a tut:Lesson, tut:Reading ; tut:order 0 ; tut:next tut:L1 ;
  tut:intro """Lesson 0 · Messages

Everything in LoopingRules is a list of statements. Each has a subject, a predicate and an object: an SPO, or triple. Even this message is: look in the sheet for a subject with lr:channel lr:out. That's how the tutorial talks to you.

You talk back the same way, by posting a message to lr:in. Type this in the box below, and press Send:

    [] a tut:Next .""" ;
  tut:hint """Type  [] a tut:Next .  in the Messages box (don't forget the final dot), and press Send.""" .

tut:L1 a tut:Lesson ; tut:order 1 ; tut:next tut:L2 ;
  tut:intro """Lesson 1 · A triple

The sheet is where state lives. Add your first triple, with the row at the bottom of the sheet:

    subject    ex:me
    predicate  a
    object     ex:Person

"a" is short for rdf:type: it says what kind of thing ex:me is. A ⚠ will appear next to it. That's expected; we'll get to it in lesson 4.""" ;
  tut:hint """Click the subject field at the bottom of the sheet, type ex:me, then a, then ex:Person, and press + Add. Any name works instead of ex:me.""" ;
  tut:praise """There it is: ex:me a ex:Person. One statement, three parts. Every fact in LoopingRules looks like this.""" .

tut:L2 a tut:Lesson ; tut:order 2 ; tut:next tut:L3 ;
  tut:intro """Lesson 2 · Properties and literals

An object can be a plain value: a literal. Give the person a name:

    ex:me  ex:name  "Ada"

The quotes make it a string; 42 would be a number, and true a boolean. Tip: click a subject in the sheet to add more properties to it.""" ;
  tut:hint """Subject ex:me, predicate ex:name, object "Ada", with the quotes.""" ;
  tut:praise """A literal is a value, not a thing, so it can't have properties of its own. Notice that the ⚠ on ex:me is gone, too.""" .

tut:L3 a tut:Lesson ; tut:order 3 ; tut:next tut:L4 ;
  tut:intro """Lesson 3 · Links

An object can also be another thing, which then has statements of its own. That's how a sheet becomes a graph. Say that you know someone, and what they are:

    ex:me     ex:knows  ex:grace
    ex:grace  a         ex:Person

There's no separate "link" concept: a link is just a triple whose object is also a subject.""" ;
  tut:hint """Two rows: (ex:me, ex:knows, ex:grace) and (ex:grace, a, ex:Person).""" ;
  tut:praise """Now ex:grace is a node of its own. Names are global: ex:grace means the same thing wherever it appears.""" .

tut:L4 a tut:Lesson ; tut:order 4 ; tut:next tut:L5 ;
  tut:intro """Lesson 4 · Shapes

See the ⚠ next to ex:grace? The model has a SHACL shape that says: every ex:Person has exactly one ex:name.

Your own edits may break a shape; the sheet just shows the violations. Operations never may: an operation whose result would break a shape isn't offered.

Make the sheet valid again: give everyone a name. (If everyone already has one, you're ahead of the tutorial.)""" ;
  tut:hint """Add (ex:grace, ex:name, "Grace"). The sheet header says ✓ valid when every shape is satisfied.""" ;
  tut:praise """Valid again. Shapes are the rules of your world, and they guard every step the engine takes.""" .

tut:L5 a tut:Lesson ; tut:order 5 ; tut:next tut:L6 ;
  tut:intro """Lesson 5 · Operations

An operation is a pattern plus a change: WHERE these triples exist, DELETE some and INSERT others. Each match is one application. The Applications panel lists them, each with the change it would make.

This model has an operation called "introduce": when someone knows someone, record that they've been introduced. It's offered now, but autopilot won't pick it, because the "rest" controller scores waiting higher.

Find "introduce" in the Applications panel, look at its preview, and apply it yourself.""" ;
  tut:hint """In the Applications panel, find "introduce" and press its Apply button. If it isn't there, check that two people with names know each other.""" ;
  tut:praise """You chose a move by hand. Autopilot does the same thing: at each tick, it applies the best-scoring valid application.""" .

tut:L6 a tut:Lesson ; tut:order 6 ; tut:next tut:L7 ;
  tut:intro """Lesson 6 · Controllers

When several applications are possible, controllers score them, and autopilot runs the best one. As this lesson started, autopilot greeted everyone formally; look for ex:greetingStyle in the sheet. It did that because the "prefer formal" controller is on.

In the Controllers panel, switch "prefer formal" off and "prefer casual" on. Then add someone new, with a name, and watch how they're greeted.""" ;
  tut:hint """Toggle the two controllers. Then add (ex:alan, a, ex:Person) and (ex:alan, ex:name, "Alan").""" ;
  tut:praise """Same model, same operations, different behaviour: you changed only the preferences. That's what controllers are for.""" .

tut:L7 a tut:Lesson ; tut:order 7 ; tut:next tut:L8 ;
  tut:intro """Lesson 7 · Commands

A message is a request, not a fact. It describes itself with its own node, and an operation decides what to do with it. Ask the model to introduce two people (use names from your sheet):

    [] a ex:Introduce ; ex:who ex:alan ; ex:to ex:me .

The model checks requests with shapes too. Try one that names something that isn't an ex:Person, and see it refused.""" ;
  tut:hint """In the Messages box, post  [] a ex:Introduce ; ex:who ex:grace ; ex:to ex:me .  Both must be an ex:Person.""" ;
  tut:praise """The request is gone. What's left is a fact: an ex:Introduction that records it. Requests come in, facts stay in the sheet, and answers go out.""" .

tut:L8 a tut:Lesson, tut:Reading ; tut:order 8 ;
  tut:intro """Lesson 8 · That's it

You've written triples, linked things, met shapes, applied an operation, steered autopilot with controllers, and sent a command. That's all of LoopingRules: the state, the rules for valid states, the moves, and the preferences, all as SPOs.

Next, open model.ttl and controllers.ttl in this folder and read how this tutorial is made: it's the same four things. Then try examples/hello-channels and examples/folder-scan.""" ;
  tut:hint """You've finished! Read model.ttl to see how the tutorial works.""" .
